<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-hypotheses" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Hypothesis Testing</title>
  <p> 
    Almost all of inferential statistics is presented as hypothesis
    testing. This section introduces the main ideas. Let me start with
    an outline. We have a data set, which is a sample of the
    population. We can calculate information about the sample: this is
    information we have. We don’t know information about the
    parameters, since they are attributes of the whole population. The
    goal of statistics is to use the sample to infer something about
    the parameter in the whole population.
  </p>
  <p>
    The setup to do this is to use the sample to make a hypothesis.
    Let me give you a definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In inferential statistics, a <term>hypothesis</term> is a
        mathematical statement about one or more population
        parameters. In hypothesis testing, there are two hypotheses.
        <ul>
          <li>
            A <term>null hypothesis</term> is a hypotheses that, in
            some way, mathematically captures a default state. Most
            often, this is a situation where two or more variables are
            not at all related, or a situation where a particular
            calculation for a single variable is not above or below an
            important threshold value.
          </li>
          <li>
            An <term>alternative hypothesis</term> is a hypotheses
            that, in some may, mathematically captures some
            interesting observation. Most often, it will say that
            there is a relatoinship between two or more variables, or
            that a particular calculation for a single variable is
            above or below an important threshold.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The hypothesis is unknown: it is a property of the whole
    population. Instead, what we know is the sample. Again, let me
    give you the important definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>sample statistics</term> is a calculation based on the
        values of variables from a sample.  In the context of
        hypothesis testing, the sample statistics should be the same
        calculation as the hypothesis, but applied to the sample
        instead of the whole population. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Now I can describe the setup of hypothesis testing. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        Based on the goal of a research study, I make two hypothesis:
        a null hypothesis and an alternative hypothesis. The null
        hypothesis says that nothing is going on with these variables,
        while the alternative hypothesis is usually a positive answer
        to the research question, indicating that something
        interesting is actually true in the population.  
      </li>
      <li>
        Then I use the sample to calculate a sample statistics.
      </li>
      <li>
        Finally, I use the mathematics of probability to determine
        whether the sample statistics supports the alternative
        hypothesis over the null hypothesis. This is usually phrase,
        sometome strangely, as a double negative: does the sample
        statistics allow me to reject the null hypothesis?
      </li>
    </ul>
    This is the setup. For most of the rest of this course, we will be
    going through these three steps for a wide variety of situations.
    For now, as usual, I’ll work through an example to demonstrate.
  </p>
  <p>
    A certain study had this research question: do male bank managers
    discriminate against female employees in promotion decisions? What
    is the population? Recall the population for a research question
    is all of the possible cases. Here, the question is about
    promotion decisions made by male managers. Therefore, the
    population is precisely that: all promotion decisions made by all
    male bank managers. (You could argue that the population be
    limited, maybe to only banks in Canada. That would also be
    reasonable.)
  </p>
  <p>
    That’s the population. What is the sample? Recall that the sample
    is the actual data: the portion of the population that we know
    something about. In this study, the sample was 48 specific
    promotion decisions that the researchers were aware of.
  </p>
  <p>
    The dataset, then, has 48 cases. Each case was two variables:
    gender and decision. Both are nominal categorical with two
    categories: gender split between men and women, and decision split
    between promoted and not promoted. The gender refers to the gender
    of the applicant: remember, that study is only about male
    managers, so that part is fixed.
  </p>
  <table xml:id="table-promotion-decisions">
    <title>Bank Promotion Decisions and Gender</title>
    <tabular>
      <row bottom="medium">>
        <cell> gender </cell>
        <cell> promoted </cell>
        <cell> not promoted </cell>
        <cell> total </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> men </cell>
        <cell> 21 </cell>
        <cell> 3 </cell>
        <cell> 24 </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="medium">
        <cell> women </cell>
        <cell> 12 </cell>
        <cell> 12</cell>
        <cell> 24 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> total </cell>
        <cell> 35 </cell>
        <cell> 13 </cell>
        <cell> 48 </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>
    <xref ref="table-promotion-decisions"/> is the contingency table
    for these two variables. Of the <m>48</m> cases, exactly half were
    men and half were women under consideration for promotion. You can
    see from the table how many were actually promoted. I can read
    this as proportions: <m>87.5</m> percent of the men were promoted and
    <m>58.3</m> percent of the women were promoted. The different between
    these two proportion is <m>0.292</m> or <m>29.2</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let’s make sense of this <m>0.292</m> proportional difference.
    Again, the population is all instances of promotion decisions made
    by a male bank manager. I can use <m>p_m</m> for the value, in the
    entire population, of how many men were promoted, as a proportion.
    (Proportion is important here for comparison, since the number of
    promotion applicants in the entire population is not guaranteed to
    be perfectly split between men and women.)
  </p>
  <p>
    Again, I can use <m>p_f</m> for the proportion of the women who
    were promoted in this situation. Then the difference, in the whole
    population, is <m>p_m - p_f</m> This is a measure of how much men
    were promoted more often than women. This is a parameter. Now I
    have two calcluations:
  </p>
  <md>
    <mrow>
      p_m - p_f = ? 
    </mrow>
    <mrow>
      \hat{p}_m - \hat{p}_f = 0.292
    </mrow>
  </md>
  <p>
    The first is a parameter, which is unknown. The second is the
    sample statistic, which is know. The question is how much the
    sample statistic actually can tell us about the parameter.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now let me setup the hypotheses.
  </p>
  <md>
    <mrow>
      H_0: p_m - p_f = 0 
    </mrow>
    <mrow>
      H_A: p_m - p_f \gt 0 
    </mrow>
  </md>
  <p>
    I’ve labeled the null hypothesis <m>H_0</m>.  It says that, in the
    whole population, there is no difference in promotion rates. It’s
    a default statement, saying that the variables of promotion and
    gender are not at all related. Then I’ve labelled the alternative
    hypothesis <m>H_A</m> It says that there is a difference in
    promotion rates, that the variables are related. In this
    particular case, the alternative hypothesis says that men are
    promoted more often than women when evalted by a male manager.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is often some variety in the statement of the alternative
    hypothesis. All three of these could be possible versions.
  </p>
  <md>
    <mrow>
      H_A: p_m - p_f \neq 0 
    </mrow>
    <mrow>
      H_A: p_m - p_f \gt 0 
    </mrow>
    <mrow>
      H_A: p_m - p_f \lt 0 
    </mrow>
  </md>
  <p>
    All three of these are negations of the null hypothesis: they all
    say that there is some relation between the variable. But they
    each are slightly different versions. The first says that the
    promotion rates are different, but has now assumption about
    whether men or women are more likely to be promoted. The second is
    explicitly looking for the possibility that men are promoted more
    and the third, likewise, for the possibility that women are
    promoted more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which of these alternative hypotheses should be used? It’s up to
    the researchers based on the situation and assumptions about the
    potential outcomes. We’ll have to keep track of this kind of
    subtlety throughout hypothesis testing, since it can affect the
    mathematics of the test.
  </p>
  <p>
    To finish this section, let me give you a list of the four most
    common types of hypotheses. Certainly not all hypothesis testing
    is one of these types, but many are.
  </p>
</section>
